+# Mobile WalletConnect deep-linking on Scaffold-ETH 2 / Base
+
+## 1. Why nothing auto-opened before the patch
+
+With WalletConnect v2, the dApp and the wallet are **two separate apps** that
+never talk directly. They share an encrypted session and communicate through
+the WalletConnect **relay** (a WebSocket server). When you call
+`writeContractAsync`, wagmi's `walletConnect` connector does this:
+
+1. Serializes the transaction as an `eth_sendTransaction` JSON-RPC request.
+2. Encrypts it with the session key and **publishes it to the relay** over the
+   WebSocket. The relay forwards it to Rainbow.
+3. Rainbow receives it and queues the signing prompt.
+
+That is why, when the user manually switches to Rainbow, **the request is
+already sitting there** — step 3 always happened. The delivery is not the
+problem.
+
+The missing piece is **foregrounding the wallet**. On mobile, the request
+arriving over the relay does *not* bring Rainbow to the front on its own (unless
+the wallet's OS-level push wakes it, which is unreliable and slow). Something
+has to switch apps, and the only thing that can is a **deep-link navigation**
+from the browser (`location.href = "rainbow://…"`).
+
+Here's the catch that breaks it: iOS/Safari (and mobile Chrome) only allow one
+app to open another **synchronously, inside the user's tap gesture** — the
+"transient user activation" window. `writeContractAsync` is asynchronous: it has
+to encrypt and round-trip the publish to the relay before it can even *know*
+which wallet to redirect to. By the time RainbowKit/WalletConnect would fire the
+redirect, the user activation from the tap has **already expired**, so the OS
+silently drops the app-switch. No error, no wallet — the page "just sits there,"
+exactly as reported, while the request quietly rides the relay into Rainbow.
+
+(Newer WalletConnect/RainbowKit versions try to auto-redirect using
+`session.peer.metadata.redirect`, but on iOS this is precisely the case that
+gets blocked when the async gap is too long.)
+
+## 2. Why the patch made it worse
+
+```js
+window.location.href = "rainbow://";   // fires first
+await writeContractAsync({ ... });     // runs second
+```
+
+The teammate correctly noticed the redirect must be in the gesture, so they put
+it first — and it works: Rainbow opens instantly. But now the **ordering is
+inverted against causality**, and it breaks in two compounding ways:
+
+- **The request doesn't exist yet.** The redirect happens *before*
+  `writeContractAsync` has published anything to the relay. Rainbow foregrounds
+  to nothing to sign.
+- **The page gets suspended before it can send.** `location.href = "rainbow://"`
+  backgrounds the browser tab. Mobile browsers **throttle or freeze JS and can
+  drop the WebSocket** in a backgrounded tab. So the `writeContractAsync` that
+  runs *after* the redirect may never execute, and even if it starts, the relay
+  publish may never flush. The request is never delivered at all — hence "the
+  transaction never lands onchain."
+
+Bare `rainbow://` also just opens the app's home screen, not a signing screen —
+there's no request bound to it.
+
+So the patch traded "request delivered but wallet doesn't open" for "wallet
+opens but request never sent." Strictly worse: before, the user could rescue it
+by switching apps manually; now there's genuinely nothing to sign.
+
+## 3. The correct pattern
+
+**Send first, foreground second, with a short delay between.**
+
+```js
+import { useAccount } from "wagmi";
+
+const { connector } = useAccount();
+
+async function onStake() {
+  // 1. Publish the request to the relay FIRST. Do not await it here — it only
+  //    resolves after the user signs, which hasn't happened yet.
+  const txPromise = writeContractAsync({ /* ...stake args... */ });
+
+  // 2. Foreground the paired wallet (mobile WC only).
+  await openWalletIfNeeded(connector);
+
+  return txPromise; // await elsewhere for the receipt / UI state
+}
+
+async function openWalletIfNeeded(connector) {
+  // --- skip conditions (see below) ---
+  if (!isMobile()) return;                      // desktop: phone gets it via relay/push
+  if (connector?.id !== "walletConnect") return; // injected / in-app browser: prompts inline
+
+  const provider = await connector.getProvider();
+  const redirect = provider?.session?.peer?.metadata?.redirect;
+  if (!redirect) return;                         // wallet gave no link — don't guess a scheme
+
+  const link = redirect.universal || redirect.native; // prefer universal on iOS
+  if (!link) return;
+
+  // 3. Let the relay finish publishing before the OS backgrounds us.
+  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 150));
+  window.location.href = link;
+}
+```
+
+**Ordering.** `writeContractAsync` is fired before the redirect so the request
+is actually on the relay by the time the wallet comes forward. This is the exact
+opposite of the patch.
+
+**The delay, and why its length matters.** The `~150 ms` pause exists to let the
+encrypted request **finish flushing over the WebSocket relay** before
+`location.href` backgrounds the page (which can suspend JS and the socket). You
+are racing two things:
+
+- Too short (the patch's ~0 ms): the app switches before the publish completes →
+  nothing to sign / never sent.
+- Too long (say >1 s): you drift outside the tap's user-activation grace window,
+  the OS blocks the app-switch, and it also just feels laggy.
+
+A small delay in the low-hundreds-of-milliseconds is the sweet spot: enough for a
+single relay round-trip to flush, short enough to keep the gesture "warm." Tune
+empirically (~100–300 ms); it's a heuristic, not an exact constant.
+
+**Which wallet scheme to open.** wagmi reports `connector.id === "walletConnect"`
+for **every** WC wallet — Rainbow, MetaMask, Trust, Zerion, all identical. So you
+**must not hardcode `rainbow://`**. Instead read the actual paired wallet from
+the live session:
+
+```
+provider.session.peer.metadata.redirect = {
+  native:    "rainbow://",              // custom scheme
+  universal: "https://rnbwapp.com/…"    // universal/app link
+}
+```
+
+Use `redirect.universal || redirect.native`. Universal links are more reliable
+than custom schemes on iOS (custom schemes can silently fail if the app isn't
+installed or the OS is in a picky state). This makes the same code correct for
+whichever wallet the user actually connected — the wallet tells you its own link.
+
+**When to skip the deep link entirely:**
+
+- **Desktop / laptop browsers.** The request rides the relay to the user's phone,
+  which surfaces it via its own notification. Redirecting the desktop browser to
+  `wallet://` does nothing (or errors). Gate on a mobile check.
+- **Injected / in-app-browser connectors** (`connector.id` is `injected`,
+  `io.metamask`, `coinbaseWallet`, or the user is inside a wallet's built-in dApp
+  browser). Here the wallet is the same runtime and prompts **inline** — a deep
+  link would either no-op or bounce the user out of context.
+- **No redirect metadata in the session.** Some wallets don't populate
+  `peer.metadata.redirect`. Don't guess a scheme — skip and let the request wait
+  (the pre-patch behavior, which at least lets the user switch manually).
+- **Desktop-initiated WC sessions in general** (QR-scanned from a browser to a
+  separate phone): never redirect the initiating device.
+
+The one-line summary: **the request must be in flight before you switch apps, the
+wallet target must come from the session's own redirect metadata (not a
+hardcoded scheme), and the switch should only happen for mobile WalletConnect
+sessions.**

